  "answer_text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia depends on the distinction between **practical control (*de facto*)** and **international legal recognition (*de jure*)**:\n\n### 1. International Legal Status (*De Jure*) \u2014 No\nAccording to international law and the vast majority of the global community, **Crimea belongs to Ukraine**. \n* **The United Nations:** Following the annexation of Crimea by Russia in March 2014, the United Nations General Assembly passed Resolution 68/262. This resolution, supported by a large majority of nations, declared the Russian-administered referendum in Crimea invalid and affirmed Ukraine\u2019s territorial integrity.\n* **Global Recognition:** The United States, the European Union, and most sovereign nations do not recognize Russia's claim to Crimea. They officially view it as a \"temporarily occupied\" territory of Ukraine.\n\n### 2. Practical Reality (*De Facto*) \u2014 Yes\nIn terms of physical control and administration, **Russia exercises full authority over Crimea**.\n* **Control:** Since deploying troops to the peninsula in early 2014, Russia has integrated Crimea into its political, economic, and legal systems. \n* **Daily Operations:** The Russian ruble is the currency, Russian law is enforced, and the local administration answers directly to Moscow. Russia considers Crimea to be two of its federal subjects: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol.\n\n### Summary of Positions\n* **Ukraine's Position:** Ukraine maintains that Crimea is an inseparable part of its sovereign territory. It views the annexation as an illegal occupation and has made the return of the peninsula to Ukrainian control a core objective.\n* **Russia's Position:** Russia claims that Crimea is a permanent and historical part of the Russian Federation. Moscow argues that the 2014 annexation was justified by a local referendum in which the majority of voters supported joining Russia, though the vote was conducted under military occupation and is widely considered illegitimate by outside observers.",
